1. MVC (Model-View-Controller) – Application Architecture
🔹 Where used:
model/ package: User, Workout, WorkoutFactory, etc.

view/ package: DashboardView, WorkoutEntryView, etc.

controller/ package: WorkoutController, DashboardFacade

🔹 Purpose:
Separates concerns:

Model: Represents data and logic (e.g., User, Workout)

View: Handles GUI (e.g., DashboardView, WorkoutEntryView)

Controller: Handles communication between view and model (WorkoutController)

✅ 2. Factory Pattern – Object Creation
🔹 Where used:
WorkoutFactory in model/WorkoutFactory.java

🔹 Purpose:
Encapsulates the logic of creating Workout objects:

java
Copy
Edit
Workout workout = workoutFactory.createWorkout(date, type, duration, calories);
Makes object creation more flexible and centralized

Useful if later you want to create different types of workouts (e.g., Cardio, Strength) using the same factory

✅ 3. Facade Pattern – Simplified Interface
🔹 Where used:
DashboardFacade in controller/DashboardFacade.java

🔹 Purpose:
Provides a unified interface to the system for the views:

java
Copy
Edit
dashboardFacade.addWorkout(...);
dashboardFacade.getProgressReport();
Hides the complexity of WorkoutController, ProgressTracker, and direct model manipulation

Keeps the UI code clean and minimal

✅ 4. Singleton Pattern (Optional / if implemented)
🔹 Potential use:
If you make DataManager or DashboardFacade a singleton:

java
Copy
Edit
public class DataManager {
    private static DataManager instance = null;
    private DataManager() {}
    public static DataManager getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) instance = new DataManager();
        return instance;
    }
}
🔹 Purpose:
Ensures only one instance of a class (e.g., central data manager, config)

Provides a global access point

(Let me know if you'd like help making any class Singleton.)

✅ 5. Builder Pattern (Optional / can be added to enhance Workout or User creation)
🔹 Potential use for complex object creation:
java
Copy
Edit
User user = new UserBuilder().setName("Alice").setEmail("a@x.com").build();
🔹 Purpose:
Builds complex objects step-by-step

Keeps constructors clean

